@startuml arch-memory-budget
' The heap decomposition of a full Google Books import and the bound that governs
' each term. The point of the picture: exactly ONE term was unbounded (the resident
' overlay), and exactly one mechanism (the eviction tail) bounds it.
' Colour legend (semantic, shared across all repo diagrams — see architecture.puml):
'   #1565C0 blue = statistical LM core   #00838F teal = storage / persistence
'   #006064 dark teal = the eviction tail
'   #546E7A grey = external crates / corpora   #C62828 red = formal verification
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/arch-memory-budget.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 1 in docs/architecture/memory-optimization.md §3
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFF3E0
skinparam NoteBorderColor #EF6C00
title Peak-heap decomposition — every term, and the mechanism that bounds it

rectangle "**Peak heap**\n<latex>H_{\text{peak}} \le H_{\text{overlay}} + H_{\text{tx}} + H_{\text{vocab}} + H_{\text{parse}} + H_{\text{alloc}}</latex>" as peak #455A64

package "The already-bounded terms" {
  rectangle "<latex>H_{\text{tx}}</latex>  —  **per-transaction buffer**\n<latex>H_{\text{tx}} \le W \cdot \text{tx\_chunk\_size} \cdot s_{\text{entry}}</latex>\nbounded by --tx-chunk-size (default 500 000)\nand by --lockfree-flush-threshold" as tx #00838F
  rectangle "<latex>H_{\text{vocab}}</latex>  —  **the vocabulary**\n<latex>\Theta(|V|)</latex>, pre-sized from a language +\nmin_count estimate ⟹ **no resize doubling**" as vocab #00838F
  rectangle "<latex>H_{\text{parse}}</latex>  —  **per-record parse**\n<latex>O(1)</latex> amortized: borrowed slices, plus a\nSmallVec token split that stays on the stack" as parse #1565C0
  rectangle "<latex>H_{\text{alloc}}</latex>  —  **allocator overhead**\nmimalloc's thread-local segment heaps\nserve from a standing reservation" as alloc #546E7A
}

package "The dominant term — the one that was unbounded" {
  rectangle "<latex>H_{\text{overlay}}</latex>  —  **resident overlay nodes**\ngrows with every //distinct// n-gram written, and\npersists for the lifetime of an open shard" as overlay #00838F
  rectangle "**Eviction tail**   (--overlay-budget-gib, default 10)\nafter each shard checkpoint, evict the coldest resident\nnodes down to that shard's slice of the global budget\n<latex>\text{budget} = \max\!\left(G / n_{\text{resident}},\ 64\,\text{MiB}\right)</latex>\n**lossless**:   evicted ⟺ durable" as evict #006064
}

rectangle "**The durable image on disk**\nCX path-compressed ARTrie + WAL\n//(unbounded by design — it is the corpus)//" as disk #00838F
rectangle "**LockFreeDurableCheckpointEviction**  (libdictenstein, TLA+)\nan evicted node's value faults back from the published\nimage, or from the retained WAL" as spec #C62828

tx -[hidden]down- vocab
vocab -[hidden]down- parse
parse -[hidden]down- alloc

tx --> peak
vocab --> peak
parse --> peak
alloc --> peak
overlay --> peak

evict -[#006064]-> overlay : reclaim resident RAM\n(≤200 000 nodes per pass)
overlay --> disk : publish snapshot
disk -[#00838F,dashed]-> overlay : fault back on read
evict -[#C62828,dashed]-> spec : losslessness is delegated here,\nnot re-proved in libgrammstein

note right of alloc
  glibc malloc serves large blocks with mmap and frees
  them with munmap; each transition issues an mprotect
  syscall to re-permission the pages. Under a 12-worker
  import that alone consumed **≈49 % of CPU**. mimalloc's
  pre-reserved segments remove the syscall from the
  steady state entirely.
end note

note bottom of evict
  **This is the load-bearing bound.** Without it, <latex>H_{\text{overlay}}</latex> grows
  monotonically for the whole import — the checkpoint //published//
  the overlay but never //reclaimed// it — and a naïve build peaks
  at **≈33.79 GB**. With it, the same build holds **under 16 GB**,
  losslessly.
end note

@enduml
